{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    Returns xs[0] + xs[1]*x + ... + xs[n]*x^n.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a single zero x such that poly(x) = 0 for a polynomial with coefficients xs.\n    Assumes an even number of coefficients guarantees a solution.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _bisection_method(func: callable, a: float, b: float, tol: float, max_iter: int) -> float:\n        # Implements the bisection method to find a root of func within [a, b].\n        # Assumes a root is bracketed and converges within max_iter.\n        for _ in range(max_iter):\n            c = (a + b) / 2\n            if abs(func(c)) < tol:\n                return c\n            if func(a) * func(c) < 0:\n                b = c\n            else:\n                a = c\n        return (a + b) / 2 # Return midpoint if max iterations reached.\n\n    # Parameters for the bisection method.\n    a, b = -1000.0, 1000.0 # Initial search interval.\n    tol = 1e-6 # Desired precision.\n    max_iter = 1000 # Maximum iterations to prevent infinite loops.\n\n    # Function to evaluate the polynomial for the bisection method.\n    func_for_bisection = lambda val: poly(xs, val)\n\n    # Check for roots at the interval boundaries.\n    if abs(func_for_bisection(a)) < tol:\n        return a\n    if abs(func_for_bisection(b)) < tol:\n        return b\n    \n    # Execute the bisection method.\n    return _bisection_method(func_for_bisection, a, b, tol, max_iter)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/35", "solution": "def max_element(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return maximum element in the list.\n    >>> max_element([1, 2, 3])\n    3\n    >>> max_element([5, 3, -5, 2, -3, 3, 9, 0, 123, 1, -10])\n    123\n    \"\"\"\n    max_val = l[0]\n    for element in l[1:]:\n        if element > max_val:\n            max_val = element\n    return max_val"}
